  "explanation": {
    "short": "In German, ei sounds like English \"eye\" (heiße), sch like \"sh\" (Schwester), w like English \"v\" (Wasser) and eu like \"oy\" (freut). ß is a sharp s. Every noun starts with a capital letter: Schwester, Wasser.",
    "more": "ie is the opposite of ei: it is a long \"ee\" (vier). German spelling is regular: once you know a few patterns, you can say any new word you read."
